From 73e152457dd1bcf86084104b08a3f460af6a7e05 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vili Kohonen <vili.kohonen@protonmail.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 10:43:06 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Preserve per-step LoRA adapters and allow resuming an
 interrupted run

Two changes needed before spending real GPU time on a 200-step run.

1. Per-checkpoint adapters

Each verl save writes both the sharded FSDP state and a small lora_adapter/
directory into the same global_step_N folder. The FSDP state is the whole base
model in fp32 - measured at 16.25 GB per save for Qwen3-4B, not the "few GB" it
sounds like. Saving every 5 steps to get a per-step view of training would
therefore mean ~40 of those, and enabling save_total_limit to control that
deletes the adapters along with the weights.

RHGRPORayTrainer._save_checkpoint now copies each step's adapter to
<output_dir>/adapters/global_step_N, outside the rotation window. That decouples
the two: save_steps drops 50 -> 5 so every 5th step is recoverable at ~250 MB
each (measured), while save_total_limit 1 keeps only the newest heavy checkpoint.
One is enough to resume a crashed run, and three would put peak disk at ~65 GB of
a 100 GB volume. Archiving failures are logged, never fatal.

2. Resume after interruption

verl's trainer.resume_mode already defaults to "auto", but it resumes from the
latest checkpoint under trainer.default_local_dir, and that path is derived from
run_id - which was always a fresh timestamp. So a restarted run got a new
directory and silently began again from step 0.

All six entrypoints now accept --run_id. Passing an existing one reuses its
directory and lets the auto-resume actually fire. Omitting it keeps the previous
timestamped behaviour.
---
 scripts/run_rl_training.py | 36 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++----
 src/train/verl/trainer.py  | 35 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 2 files changed, 66 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/scripts/run_rl_training.py b/scripts/run_rl_training.py
